FAQ = [
 ("What is the best tonneau cover for a 2019+ Silverado 1500?",
  "For most trucks, the BAKFlip MX4 (448130 for the short bed). RealTruck lists it at $1,199.99 with a 400 lb distributed load rating, a 5-year warranty, a sub-30-minute install and a fold that doesn't block the third brake light. If you want a lockable hard cover for about half that, the Gator EFX at $599 is the value pick. If you open the bed many times a day and want it fully open, look at the RetraxPRO MX."),
 ("Do covers for a 2014–2018 Silverado fit a 2019 Silverado 1500?",
  "Only if the 2019 is an LD. The 2019 Silverado 1500 LD was the previous-generation truck built alongside the new T1, so it takes 2014–2018 covers (listings say \"2019 Limited/Legacy\"). The all-new 2019 truck has a different, larger bed and needs a cover listed for 2019–2026. Tyger, for example, sells TG-BC3C1006 for 2014–2018 and 2019 LD, and TG-BC3C1053 for the new body, which excludes the LD."),
 ("Is the 2022 Silverado 1500 LTD the same as the 2019 LD for covers?",
  "No, and this trips people up. The 2022 Silverado 1500 LTD was the pre-refresh T1 truck (the same as a 2021) sold alongside the refreshed 2022. It has the T1 bed, so 2019–2026 covers fit it. Tyger's T3 fitment names the 2022 Silverado 1500 LTD as compatible. Only the 2019 LD uses the old bed."),
 ("How do I tell which Silverado bed I have?",
  "Measure the floor from the front bulkhead to the inside of the closed tailgate. About 70 in (69.9) is the short bed, which comes only on the Crew Cab. About 79 in (79.4) is the standard bed, sold on Double Cab and Crew Cab. About 98 in (98.2) is the long bed. Retailers label the short bed 5'8\" or 5'10\", and the standard bed 6'6\" or 6'7\". Go by the inches, not the label."),
 ("Will a tonneau cover work with the Multi-Flex tailgate?",
  "Yes, with the covers on this page. BAK, Retrax, TruXedo and UnderCover all state their Silverado part numbers work with or without the Multi-Flex (GMC calls it MultiPro). The practical catch is the inner gate: with a folding cover latched shut, the inner section of the tailgate is trapped under the tailgate seal, so you fold the rear panel first if you want to use the step or the inner gate load stop."),
 ("Does a tonneau cover work with the power up/down tailgate?",
  "Check each listing. Tyger states that equipping the T3 \"will interfere with power tailgate.\" GM offered power-down and power up/down tailgates on the T1, the latter on LTZ and High Country. The Multi-Flex tailgate and the power tailgate were mutually exclusive, so if you have Multi-Flex you don't have power operation. If you have a power tailgate, confirm with the seller before ordering a cover not listed for it."),
 ("Do these covers fit trucks with GM's in-bed storage boxes?",
  "No. Every RealTruck fitment note on this page, from the BAKFlip MX4 and Gator EFX to the RetraxPRO MX and TruXedo Lo Pro, says it will not work with factory side storage boxes. The boxes sit where the cover's side rails clamp. If you have them, you need either a cover built around toolboxes or to remove the boxes."),
 ("Hard or soft cover for a Silverado?",
  "Hard covers (MX4, Ultra Flex, EFX, RetraxPRO) lock under a locked tailgate and carry 300–500 lb spread evenly. Soft covers (Lo Pro, T3) cost $249–$520, go on in minutes and come off without tools, but vinyl stops weather and prying eyes, not a knife. On a truck that sits outside with tools in the bed, pay for hard."),
 ("Can I use a Silverado cover on a GMC Sierra?",
  "Yes on a steel bed. Every part number on this page is sold as Silverado/Sierra 1500 for 2019–2026, and the beds are shared. The exception is a Sierra with the CarbonPro composite bed: the BAKFlip MX4 448130, RetraxPRO MX 80481, TruXedo Lo Pro 572401 and Tyger T3 all exclude it. Our Sierra 1500 guide covers the CarbonPro-specific part numbers."),
 ("Can I add a bed rack with a tonneau cover on a Silverado?",
  "Not with a standard folding cover, which leaves nothing to bolt a rack to. Retrax sells the RetraxPRO XR (T-80481 on the short bed) with T-slot rails along both sides, so you can mount crossbars or a rack with the cover in place. Trail Boss owners on TrailBoss.org make the same point: a hard rolling cover leaves room for a bed rack or rail system later."),
]

